(D1 — Discrepancy 1 repro: on 0.4.9 and earlier, a global declared BELOW
 a function was read inside that function as a raw machine word unless
 it was a `number`. LANGUAGE.md:757 says top-level variables "are global
 and can be used inside functions" — the same sentence now also states
 the fixed rule directly (0.4.10). The same five globals, read at top
 level, all printed correctly; read from a function defined above them,
 only the number survived. The text and buffer addresses were static
 rodata/heap addresses and the float was its IEEE-754 bit pattern, so
 every wrong answer looked like data. The list and buffer addresses vary
 per run (ASLR), so this probe is compared on its EXIT CODE only — it
 always exits 3, whichever way the in-function reads come out; the
 printed lines below the "Ran (values seen ...)" note are the record of
 what actually happened at each version.
 RESOLVED, 2026-08-22 — vox #66 ("Codegen now reads every top-level
 declaration's type in a pre-pass and gives it to each function body
 before generating it, so a global is read as its declared type wherever
 the declaration sits").
  Ran (values seen 2026-08-21, pre-0.4.9): in-function reads printed
    42 / 4210888 / 4612811918334230528 / 140547700088832 / 140547700080640
  Ran (values seen 2026-08-22, vox 0.4.9, byte-identical shape): in-function
    reads printed 42 / 4210888 / 4612811918334230528 / <2 varying addresses>
  against top-level 42 / hello / 2.5 / [1, 2, 3] / ABC, both times.
  Ran (values seen 2026-08-22, vox 0.4.10, FIXED): in-function reads now
    print 42 / hello / 2.5 / [1, 2, 3] / ABC — byte-identical to top-level.
 Ran: VOX_CORE_PATH=/home/josj/scr/english/vox/coreasm /home/josj/scr/english/vox/target/release/vox D1.vox -o p && ./p
 expected output:
   exit 3
)
